<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

    ## SECTION 1 — HEADER BANNER
    <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # First, I Cut the Paper. – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    ## SECTION 2 — LESSON IDENTIFICATION TABLE
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Following Instructions at School</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Cut the Paper.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write thank-you cards. Interactive: Respond to written questions about a class reading assignment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a simple thank-you card to a classmate or teacher for helping with a class project (e.g., "Thank you for helping me cut the paper for the poster."). Can answer simple written questions about steps in a class project (e.g., "What did you cut?" "I cut the paper for the poster." "What did you use to glue the pictures?" "I used glue."). Can write short answers about following instructions in class (e.g., "Where do we have to put the poster?" "On the wall in the classroom." "What did you do after cutting the paper?" "I glued it on the poster.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    ## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows realia: a piece of paper, scissors, glue, and crayons. Students identify the objects. The teacher asks, "What can we make with these?" and elicits ideas like "poster," "mask," "card." The teacher writes student ideas on the board. Students then write down one thing they can make with these items in their notebooks.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a simple thank-you card and says, "Today, we will learn to write thank-you cards." The teacher writes "Thank you for helping me cut the paper!" on the board. The teacher points to 'cut' and 'paper', emphasizing the /p/ sound in "paper" and the /s/ sound in "scissors" (by showing the scissors). The teacher also models sequencing words by writing, "First, I cut. Next, I glue."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we going to draw or write today?" (Expected answer: Write.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What type of card are we going to write?" (Expected answer: Thank-you card.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Following Instructions at School".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short procedural text titled "How to Make a Small Poster for Our Classroom." The text is displayed on a projector or large chart paper, using simple sentences and incorporating target vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, poster, classroom, cut, paste) and sequencing words (first, next, then). The text also uses simple imperatives. For example:</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">"<b>How to Make a Small Poster for Our Classroom</b><br>
                    <b>First</b>, get your paper, scissors, and glue. <br>
                    <b>Next</b>, cut a big square of paper. <br>
                    <b>Then</b>, paste a small picture on the square. <br>
                    <b>After that</b>, write a fun word on the poster. <br>
                    <b>Finally</b>, put your poster on the wall in the classroom."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Producing written texts for specific purposes (giving instructions, expressing gratitude).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text silently. The teacher asks written questions about the text on the board. Students write short answers in their notebooks:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you get first?" (Expected answer: Paper, scissors, glue.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you cut?" (Expected answer: A big square of paper.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where do you put the poster?" (Expected answer: On the wall in the classroom.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice writing sentences using sequencing words (first, next, then, after that, finally), plural imperatives ("Let's make a poster."), future tense ("I will make a mask," "I am going to make a mask"), and target vocabulary (paper, scissors, glue, poster, classroom, cut, paste, write).</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks. Students complete them using appropriate sequencing words or vocabulary. Example: "___, get your ____ and scissors. Next, ___ the paper." (First, paper, cut). The teacher also provides sentences like "I _____ make a mask." or "I ______ ______ make a mask." (will, am going to).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Reordering):</b> Students are given scrambled sentences related to making something (e.g., "scissors / paper / cut / the / first"). In pairs, they rewrite the sentences in the correct order in their notebooks: "First, cut the paper with scissors." They also practice writing simple imperatives like "Fold the paper!" and "Paste the picture!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Guided Sentence Construction:</b> The teacher shows pictures of simple steps for making a small card (e.g., Picture 1: a child drawing, Picture 2: a child cutting, Picture 3: a child gluing). In pairs, students write one sentence for each picture using sequencing words and target vocabulary. Example: "First, draw a picture. Next, cut the paper. Then, glue the parts."</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Following Instructions at School".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written Product - Students will write short thank-you cards and respond to written questions based on a simulated class project. This connects directly to the scenario of "Following Instructions at School" and the theme "First, I Cut the Paper."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Thank-You Card Writing:</b> Students imagine they worked on a class project (like making a poster or mask) with a classmate or the teacher. They will each write a simple thank-you card. The teacher provides small pieces of paper for cards. On the card, students should include:
                            <ul>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">A greeting (e.g., "Dear [Name],")</li>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">A thank-you message for specific help (e.g., "Thank you for helping me cut the paper for the poster!" or "Thank you for the colorful crayons.").</li>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">Their name.</li>
                            </ul>
                            They are encouraged to use vocabulary like 'cut', 'glue', 'paper', 'poster', 'crayon', 'colorful', and 'project'. They can also write a future intention like, "Next week, I will make a mask with you!"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Responding to Written Questions:</b> After writing the cards, the teacher displays three written questions on the board related to the imagined project. Students write short answers in their notebooks, demonstrating their understanding of following instructions.
                            <ul>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">"What did you do after cutting the paper?" (Expected answer: "I glued it on the poster.")</li>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">"What did you use to draw the picture?" (Expected answer: "I used a pencil/crayon.")</li>
                                <li style="list-style-type: circle; margin-left: 20px; text-align: justify;">"Where do we put the finished project?" (Expected answer: "On the wall in the classroom.")</li>
                            </ul>
                        </li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, accurate use of target vocabulary and sequencing words, and effective communication of gratitude and project steps.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist while students are writing, focusing on the use of target grammar and vocabulary. Peer assessment of thank-you cards and written answers.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students submit their self-created thank-you cards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students exchange their thank-you cards with a partner. Partners read each other's cards.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students' written answers to the questions from Stage 4 are collected.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and completeness of the thank-you message.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of target vocabulary (e.g., paper, cut, glue, poster, classroom).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of sequencing words (first, next) in answers about instructions.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility of writing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to answer simple written questions accurately.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy to write today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult to write today?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <b style="color: #1a5276;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</b>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did you learn about writing instructions or thank-you cards today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can you use sequencing words (first, next) when you write at home?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students successfully wrote simple thank-you cards and short answers. The use of realia helped activate vocabulary. Some students needed more support with spelling 'scissors' and 'classroom', which are compound words. Next time, I will provide a sentence frame for the thank-you card to scaffold weaker writers and ensure they include more of the target vocabulary. I will also review compound words like 'classroom' and 'cardboard' explicitly for phonemic awareness.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on writing. The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will consolidate all skills by having students work on a collaborative project where they use the instructions learned, potentially creating a small poster or mask, and explaining their process to others, integrating speaking, listening, reading, and writing in a final 21st Century Skills project.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>